1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,648
Welcome to Wild Duck, a story by Romesh Gunesekera.

2
00:00:03,868 --> 00:00:08,988
You have read it already; now let us retrace what happens and why the ending matters.

3
00:00:09,208 --> 00:00:12,941
A father and son sit in a motionless boat on a wilderness lake.

4
00:00:13,161 --> 00:00:14,505
They both have guns.

5
00:00:14,725 --> 00:00:19,952
The father points towards a red volcanic hill, where he expects the wild ducks to appear.

6
00:00:20,172 --> 00:00:23,671
The boy has come to shoot, but watching birds is his passion.

7
00:00:23,891 --> 00:00:29,628
Keep that difference in mind: at first, what interests the boy most—the gun, or the living flock?

8
00:00:29,848 --> 00:00:34,349
Pause and say it aloud: where are the father and son, and what are they waiting for?

9
00:00:41,849 --> 00:00:46,585
They are in a small boat on a lake, waiting for wild ducks—teal—to fly over.

10
00:00:46,805 --> 00:00:49,365
The oars are tucked in, so the boat stays still.

11
00:00:50,385 --> 00:00:55,143
Light changes across the flat water: orange, red and mauve seep from the sky.

12
00:00:55,363 --> 00:00:56,835
It is late afternoon.

13
00:00:57,055 --> 00:01:00,255
Suddenly the boy sees a ribbon of black dots approaching.

14
00:01:00,475 --> 00:01:04,421
He is spellbound—so fascinated that he almost forgets to breathe.

15
00:01:04,641 --> 00:01:06,945
Huge numbers of teal fill half the sky.

16
00:01:07,165 --> 00:01:11,923
They bank, or tilt and turn, and smaller groups dive and rise in formations.

17
00:01:12,143 --> 00:01:16,473
A formation is an organised pattern made by birds flying together.

18
00:01:16,693 --> 00:01:20,917
Notice how the scene invites us to admire the flock before anyone shoots.

19
00:01:21,137 --> 00:01:24,700
What does spellbound tell you about the boy's reaction to the birds?

20
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:34,483
He is captivated and full of wonder.

21
00:01:34,703 --> 00:01:38,799
Birdwatching is his passion; the flock is more than a target to him.

22
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:44,661
The father tells his son to be quick, aim ahead of the birds, and manage with one shot.

23
00:01:44,881 --> 00:01:48,252
When the teal pass overhead, the father fires repeatedly.

24
00:01:48,472 --> 00:01:53,656
The explosions shake the boat and ripple the lake, but he says the birds are flying too high.

25
00:01:53,876 --> 00:01:56,159
The boy's movements are slower and steadier.

26
00:01:56,379 --> 00:02:00,837
He picks one bird out of the great mass, a leader in a formation, and fires.

27
00:02:01,057 --> 00:02:04,215
The bird plummets: it drops sharply towards the water.

28
00:02:04,435 --> 00:02:07,784
Another bird immediately takes its place in the formation.

29
00:02:08,004 --> 00:02:11,375
The father cheers, but the boy wants to find the fallen duck.

30
00:02:11,595 --> 00:02:15,179
Who hits a bird, and what happens to the flock when that bird falls?

31
00:02:22,679 --> 00:02:24,172
The boy hits one bird.

32
00:02:24,392 --> 00:02:28,637
It falls, but another bird takes its place and the flock continues flying.

33
00:02:28,857 --> 00:02:32,761
The father fired too, but the story does not say that he hit a bird.

34
00:02:33,781 --> 00:02:38,347
The father directs the boy towards the bulrushes, the tall reeds near the water.

35
00:02:38,567 --> 00:02:40,423
The boy paddles against the tide.

36
00:02:40,643 --> 00:02:41,901
His arms tremble.

37
00:02:42,121 --> 00:02:49,097
He sees a shape floating there: the duck has a broken wing, described as ragged, like the torn sail of a toy boat.

38
00:02:49,317 --> 00:02:50,469
He scoops it up.

39
00:02:50,689 --> 00:02:53,847
The father says, “Well done.” Yet the boy is silent.

40
00:02:54,067 --> 00:02:59,399
Touching the soft feathers and the thin, bony neck makes the duck real to him in a new way.

41
00:02:59,619 --> 00:03:01,581
He looks frightened by what he has done.

42
00:03:01,801 --> 00:03:04,617
Why does “Well done” fail to make the boy happy?

43
00:03:12,117 --> 00:03:14,421
He sees and touches the wounded bird.

44
00:03:14,641 --> 00:03:19,655
His attention shifts from making a successful shot to the suffering of a living creature.

45
00:03:20,675 --> 00:03:22,275
Then the duck lifts its head.

46
00:03:22,495 --> 00:03:23,732
It is not dead.

47
00:03:23,952 --> 00:03:26,107
The boy breathes in relief and strokes it.

48
00:03:26,327 --> 00:03:27,969
He says he wants to go home.

49
00:03:28,189 --> 00:03:33,181
The father tells him he will have to look after the bird, and the boy answers that he wants to.

50
00:03:33,401 --> 00:03:34,916
This is the turning point.

51
00:03:35,136 --> 00:03:38,421
Relief does not erase what happened: the wing is broken.

52
00:03:38,641 --> 00:03:42,311
But the boy now chooses responsibility for the duck he injured.

53
00:03:42,531 --> 00:03:46,115
What two feelings can the boy have at once when the duck lifts its head?

54
00:03:53,615 --> 00:03:58,500
He can feel relieved that the duck is alive and upset or guilty that he wounded it.

55
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:01,173
Both feelings fit his actions in the story.

56
00:04:02,193 --> 00:04:06,396
At home, the boy bandages the wing and learns how to feed the maimed duck.

57
00:04:06,616 --> 00:04:08,451
Maimed means seriously injured.

58
00:04:08,671 --> 00:04:11,871
He spends hours watching it waddle around the backyard.

59
00:04:12,091 --> 00:04:16,293
Sometimes it stretches its neck and tries to flap its lopsided wings.

60
00:04:16,513 --> 00:04:20,332
Lopsided means uneven, with one side different from the other.

61
00:04:20,552 --> 00:04:24,883
The text is clear about the lasting result: the bird can never fly again.

62
00:04:25,103 --> 00:04:29,839
Caring for it does not undo the injury, but it shows how deeply the boy has changed.

63
00:04:30,059 --> 00:04:33,515
What does the boy do for the duck, and what can the duck no longer do?

64
00:04:41,015 --> 00:04:44,001
He bandages its wing, feeds it and looks after it.

65
00:04:44,221 --> 00:04:47,315
Because its wing is broken, it can never fly again.

66
00:04:48,335 --> 00:04:50,916
One day the father asks where the boy's gun is.

67
00:04:51,136 --> 00:04:57,109
The boy says he threw it away, defiantly—boldly, even though he expects his father to object.

68
00:04:57,329 --> 00:04:58,673
The father is shocked.

69
00:04:58,893 --> 00:05:01,752
The boy insists that the duck is his to care for.

70
00:05:01,972 --> 00:05:07,860
The father argues that the gun gave him the duck; the boy replies that using it made him feel sick.

71
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:11,045
He cannot separate that object from the harm he caused.

72
00:05:11,265 --> 00:05:14,913
Then the father's anger gives way to relief and a sense of peace.

73
00:05:15,133 --> 00:05:17,800
The story lets us see a change in both of them.

74
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:20,153
Why does the boy throw away his gun?

75
00:05:20,653 --> 00:05:23,576
Answer with a feeling and a reason from the story.

76
00:05:31,076 --> 00:05:35,961
He feels sick about injuring the duck and no longer wants the gun that caused its suffering.

77
00:05:36,181 --> 00:05:38,912
Looking after the duck has deepened his compassion.

78
00:05:39,932 --> 00:05:42,385
Let us collect the words that carry the story.

79
00:05:42,605 --> 00:05:45,315
Spellbound: completely fascinated by the flock.

80
00:05:45,535 --> 00:05:48,500
Formation: the pattern of birds flying together.

81
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:50,853
Plummeted: fell quickly from the sky.

82
00:05:51,073 --> 00:05:53,719
Bulrushes: tall plants growing by the water.

83
00:05:53,939 --> 00:05:56,883
Ragged: torn and uneven, like the injured wing.

84
00:05:57,103 --> 00:05:58,916
Maimed: seriously injured.

85
00:05:59,136 --> 00:06:00,800
Lopsided: uneven.

86
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:04,561
Defiantly: standing by a decision despite opposition.

87
00:06:04,781 --> 00:06:09,133
Try saying two of these words in your own sentence about the boy and the duck.

88
00:06:09,353 --> 00:06:13,791
Which word means “fell quickly,” and which describes the duck's uneven wings?

89
00:06:21,291 --> 00:06:22,955
Plummeted means fell quickly.

90
00:06:23,175 --> 00:06:25,607
Lopsided describes the uneven wings.

91
00:06:25,827 --> 00:06:28,493
Ragged describes the torn-looking injured wing.

92
00:06:29,513 --> 00:06:31,775
Now tell the story back in five steps.

93
00:06:31,995 --> 00:06:34,299
First, the father and son wait on the lake.

94
00:06:34,519 --> 00:06:37,228
Second, the boy watches the remarkable flock.

95
00:06:37,448 --> 00:06:39,091
Third, he shoots one duck.

96
00:06:39,311 --> 00:06:43,980
Fourth, he discovers it is alive but badly injured and cares for it at home.

97
00:06:44,200 --> 00:06:46,097
Fifth, he throws away his gun.

98
00:06:46,317 --> 00:06:52,525
The sequence matters because his decision at the end grows from what he saw, touched and did after the shot.

99
00:06:52,745 --> 00:06:54,431
Pause for as long as you need.

100
00:06:54,931 --> 00:06:58,536
What is the moment when the boy begins to think differently about shooting?

101
00:07:06,036 --> 00:07:09,833
Finding and touching the helpless, injured duck changes his view.

102
00:07:10,053 --> 00:07:14,235
Its lifting its head brings relief, and caring for it strengthens that change.

103
00:07:15,255 --> 00:07:17,239
Here are three ideas to remember.

104
00:07:17,459 --> 00:07:21,747
The boy begins as someone who loves watching birds, yet he is there to shoot.

105
00:07:21,967 --> 00:07:27,129
Seeing the wounded duck turns a distant bird into a creature he feels responsible for.

106
00:07:27,349 --> 00:07:32,939
He cares for it and rejects his gun, while his father is relieved rather than angry in the end.

107
00:07:33,159 --> 00:07:38,364
Wild Duck asks us to notice how direct experience can change a person's choices.

108
00:07:38,584 --> 00:07:46,029
When you revise, use precise details—the moving formation, the broken wing and the missing gun—to support that idea.

109
00:07:46,249 --> 00:07:50,793
Final quick question: what detail best shows that the father has changed too?

110
00:07:58,293 --> 00:08:01,536
After learning the gun is gone, he feels relief and peace.

111
00:08:01,756 --> 00:08:06,172
The text shows that reaction, though it does not tell us what he will do in the future.

112
00:08:06,392 --> 00:08:07,864
You have finished the revision.

113
00:08:08,084 --> 00:08:13,439
If one answer felt difficult, revisit that scene or the corresponding page in the chapter.

114
00:08:13,659 --> 00:08:19,269
Then try telling the whole story in your own words, from the still boat to the father's feeling of peace.
